  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 340 AM CDT Tue Sep 1 2026\n\nRidge of high pressure will break down slightly today and give\nway to a weak upper level wave moving through in southwest flow.\nWith the lack of temperature advection to the area, temperatures\nlook to remain near to perhaps slightly below normal today with\nhighs in the 70s to lower 80s. The mentioned upper level wave\ndoes look to bring some decent vorticity advection this\nafternoon through this evening. Hi-res models indicating some\nshower activity is possible as a result, mainly along and south\nof Interstate 94. There is an abundance of dry air this wave\nwould have to overcome, and the better moisture plume looks to\nstay to our south. Confidence is low with shower activity today,\nalthough hi-res guidance is consistent on placing in some\nshowers. Thus added slight pops to these southern areas this\nafternoon through much of the evening. The chance for\nthunderstorms today is fairly low given the lack of instability.\nShower activity then diminishes late this evening with overnight\nlows in the 50s. Light winds and high RH recoveries could bring\nsome patchy fog tonight into Wednesday morning, provided some\nclearing of clouds occurs. Brief ridging aloft then moves\nthrough for Wednesday, keeping mainly dry conditions. A switch\nto breezy southerly flow at the surface could bring start a\nwarming trend with highs in the mid 70s to mid 80s. Wednesday\nevening through Wednesday night, surface low kicks out of the\nnorthern Rockies and brings a weak warm front across the area.\nThis could return more broad chances for showers and\nthunderstorms across the state. There is a decent amount of\nelevated instability with this wave, while the mix and surface\nlayers have limited instability and strong capping. Shear also\nlooks low with this wave. Give the later timing of this wave the\nsevere threat is overall low with only an isolated strong storm\npossible. Lows Wednesday night will be warming and generally\nnear 60. Shower and thunderstorm chances could linger in the\nnorth and east on Thursday as the warm front continues to move\nthrough. The severe weather threat again looks low given the\nsame reasons previously discussed. Ridging aloft then brings \ndry conditions, while the resultant warm sector behind the warm \nfront continues to warm temperatures in the upper 70s to upper \n80s. Mainly dry and mild conditions are then expected for\nThursday night with lows again near 60 degrees.\n\nFriday through the Labor day weekend...ridging aloft will\nprovide southwest flow at times through the holiday weekend.\nHigh temperatures look to stay above normal, and perhaps in the\nlower 90s, through at least Saturday. Sunday onward sees a\ncooling trend in forecast temperatures, although spreads remain\nlarge. This is from the uncertainty on the influence of a large\npacific northwest trough, possibly moving inland late this\nweekend or early next week. Shower and thunderstorm chances look\nlimited to start the weekend, although the southwest flow could\nbring weak disturbances at times. Increased precipitation\nchances may come later in the weekend into early next week\ndepending on the progression of the upper level trough. Of note\ncould be some increase monsoonal moisture with this next trough,\npossibly providing some localized increased rain totals. \nHowever, this is highly dependent on where this trough tracks \nearly next week. Lastly, fire weather concerns may be slightly \nelevated to end the week due to warm temperatures and some dry \nair lingering in the west. RH values look to improve later in \nthe weekend, helping to limit the fire weather danger.",
